From: linux-ntfs

Subject: [PATCH] ntfs: allow colon (':') in file names

ntfs_check_bad_char() unconditionally rejects ':' (0x003A) when
creating, renaming, or linking files, regardless of mount options.
Unlike '/' (which can never appear in a path component) or '\\'
(reserved for short-name/DOS compatibility), a raw colon in the
on-disk filename attribute is perfectly valid NTFS content -- the
restriction against it is purely a Win32 API/Explorer convention
(colon is used to introduce an alternate data stream name), not an
on-disk filesystem limitation, and this driver does not implement
colon-separated ADS access via the file name.

Drop the 0x003A comparison so colons are always permitted. All other
Windows-reserved characters ('"', '*', '/', '<', '>', '?', '\\', '|'
and control characters) remain blocked unconditionally, and the
windows_names mount option still applies its other checks (reserved
device names, trailing space/dot) unchanged.
---
--- a/fs/ntfs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/ntfs/namei.c
@@ -49,7 +49,7 @@

 		if (c < 0x0020 ||
 		    c == 0x0022 || c == 0x002A || c == 0x002F ||
-		    c == 0x003A || c == 0x003C || c == 0x003E ||
+		    c == 0x003C || c == 0x003E ||
 		    c == 0x003F || c == 0x005C || c == 0x007C)
 			return -EINVAL;
 	}
